<!--
    AML endpoint table.

    AML destinations are run by national emergency services. Most of them
    were agreed privately between the PSAP, the mobile operators and the
    big OS vendors, and are not published anywhere. Sending an emergency
    location to the wrong place helps nobody, so the rule here is simple:

      * A country only gets an <sms> or <https> line when an official or
        primary source says where AML goes. Every entry cites its source.
      * Countries we care about but could not find a public endpoint for
        are listed with their triggers only. They do nothing until someone
        adds an endpoint obtained from the PSAP operator.

    You can replace this table without touching the app:
      * drop a file at /product/etc/cipheraml/aml_endpoints.xml or
        /system_ext/etc/cipheraml/aml_endpoints.xml, or
      * overlay this resource with an RRO.

    Format:

    <country name="..."          Free text, only used in logs and Settings.
             mcc="234,235"       Network MCCs this entry applies to.
             iso="gb"            Country ISO codes, used when the MCC is
                                 not known yet (limited service).
             confidence="90">    Optional, overrides config_amlConfidencePercent.

        <trigger number="999" /> Numbers that start AML. Leave out every
        <trigger number="112" /> trigger to start on any emergency number.

        <sms destination="999"   Where the SMS goes.
             type="text"         "text" (default) or "data".
             port="..."          Destination port, only for type="data".
             optional-fields="false"  Also send al= and toc= when they fit.
             partial-imei="false" />  Send only the TAC part of the IMEI.

        <https url="https://..." />  Annex B HTTPS endpoint. https only.
    </country>

    Sources referred to below:
      [ETSI]  ETSI TS 103 625 V1.2.1, Transporting Handset Location to
              PSAPs for Emergency Communications, Advanced Mobile Location.
      [EENA]  EENA, AML Report Card 2023 Update. Per country questionnaire
              answered by the national authorities.
-->
<aml-endpoints version="1">

    <!--
        United Kingdom.

        AML SMS goes to 999 and is picked up by BT, which runs the Stage 1
        PSAP and the AML end-point. SMS to 999 for AML is zero rated.

          [ETSI] clause 6.3.4.1 refers to "the normal 999 code for UK AML
                 messages".
          [EENA] United Kingdom, 3.1: SMS to short number for Android and
                 iOS. 3.3: zero rated. 3.4: BT Stage 1 PSAP.
                 1.6: AML is activated for 112 and 999.

        Jersey, Guernsey and the Isle of Man share MCC 234 but run their
        own emergency services, so AML there may not reach BT.
    -->
    <country name="United Kingdom" mcc="234,235" iso="gb">
        <trigger number="999" />
        <trigger number="112" />
        <sms destination="999" />
    </country>

    <!--
        Slovakia.

        AML SMS goes to 112 and is handled by the Ministry of Interior.
        Zero rated.

          [EENA] Slovakia, 3.1: SMS to short number for Android, iOS and
                 HarmonyOS, additional details "SMS to 112 number".
                 3.3: zero rated. 3.4: Ministry of Interior of the Slovak
                 Republic. 1.6: AML is activated for 112, 150 (fire and
                 rescue), 155 (emergency medical) and 158 (police).
    -->
    <country name="Slovakia" mcc="231" iso="sk">
        <trigger number="112" />
        <trigger number="150" />
        <trigger number="155" />
        <trigger number="158" />
        <sms destination="112" />
    </country>
</aml-endpoints>
